<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iltani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">18th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Iltani was a queen of Karana in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> in the 18th century BCE, wife of King Aqba-hammu, who ruled as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. Some 200 tablets from her archive, found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-al-rimah">Tell al-Rimah</a>, record her management of textile workshops, grain, staff and estates and her letters with relatives, giving a rare view of a royal woman's economic role.</p></div>
